# Package digests are computed over DELIVERED BYTES (spec D3.1), so git must
# hand every platform the same bytes it handed the machine that digested them.
#
# The corpus bundles were written on Windows in text mode: their manifests
# digest CRLF bytes, while git stored the blobs LF-normalised (autocrlf).
# Without this file a clone on Linux/macOS - or any checkout with
# core.autocrlf=false/input - fails every package digest (6,914 objects +
# 49 pinned facet schemas at 1.0.0; semantic digests are unaffected, which
# is the two-digest design working, but the validator rightly reports the
# package mismatch). `eol=crlf` reproduces the digested bytes on checkout
# without re-spelling a single published digest. The converters write
# these bytes explicitly (oscal_conv_lib.BUNDLE_NEWLINE), so a re-run is
# byte-identical on every OS.
#
# Measured (2026-11-10, register "Amendments - CP-RISK-1", finding L-1):
# fresh clones under core.autocrlf=true/false/input, an existing
# Windows-style tree receiving this file, and `git archive` (what a ZIP
# download is built from) all verify 6,914/6,914 objects + 49/49 pins.
# KNOWN LIMIT: the blobs themselves stay LF, so a consumer that bypasses
# checkout (raw blob URLs, `git show`, a client that ignores eol
# attributes) still sees a package mismatch with a semantic match. The
# cure for that channel is storing the CRLF bytes as blobs under `-text`
# (measured: a 6,975-file commit, pack +0.02 MiB) - not done here because
# nothing in the repository reads blobs that way and the commit would bury
# every other change under review. Backlog #42.
converted_examples/*/*-core-bundle/** text eol=crlf

# The skill's example bundle ships inside SKILL_jascon.zip with LF bytes;
# its content-manifest digests those. Keep them LF on Windows checkouts too.
jascon/examples/*.json text eol=lf
